export default "# DOCKER AND CONTAINERS: DOCKERFILES AND BUILDS\n\n# PART 1 OF 4: UNDERSTANDING DOCKERFILES AND IMAGE BUILDS\n\n## 1. Introduction to Docker\n\nBefore learning Dockerfiles, we need to understand why containers exist.\n\nImagine developing a Node.js application on your computer.\n\nYour application requires:\n\n- Node.js.\n- npm.\n- Application dependencies.\n- Environment variables.\n- Configuration files.\n- A particular operating system environment.\n\nYou deploy the application to a Linux VPS.\n\nHowever, the server has a different Node.js version. Some dependencies behave differently, and the application fails.\n\nYou might encounter:\n\n```text\nError: Cannot find module 'express'\n```\n\nOr:\n\n```text\nError: Unsupported engine\n```\n\nOr:\n\n```text\nApplication works locally but fails on production.\n```\n\nThese problems can result from differences between development and production environments.\n\nDocker helps address this problem by packaging an application and its required runtime environment into a container image.\n\n### What is Docker?\n\nDocker is a platform and ecosystem for building, distributing, and running containerized applications.\n\nIt allows developers to package applications into standardized images.\n\nThese images can then be used to create containers across compatible environments.\n\n### What is a container?\n\nA container is an isolated running instance of an image.\n\nFor example:\n\n```text\nDocker Image\n     |\n     v\nDocker Container\n     |\n     v\nRunning Application\n```\n\nAn image is the package or template.\n\nA container is an instance created from that image.\n\nYou can create multiple containers from the same image.\n\n### Real-world example\n\nSuppose you have a marketplace backend.\n\nInstead of manually installing Node.js and dependencies on every server, you build a Docker image containing the application runtime and required files.\n\nYou can run that image on:\n\n- Your development computer.\n- A Linux VPS.\n- A cloud virtual machine.\n- A container hosting service.\n- A Kubernetes cluster.\n\nThe exact host configuration still matters, but the container image provides a consistent application environment.\n\n## 2. Understanding the Docker Architecture\n\nDocker uses several components that work together.\n\n| Component | Responsibility |\n|---|---|\n| Docker Client | Sends commands to Docker |\n| Docker Daemon | Manages Docker objects and operations |\n| Docker Engine | Provides the container management environment |\n| Dockerfile | Defines image-building instructions |\n| Docker Image | Read-only application package |\n| Container | Running instance of an image |\n| Docker Registry | Stores and distributes images |\n| Build Context | Files available to the image builder |\n\n### Docker architecture\n\n```text\n             DEVELOPER\n                 |\n                 v\n            DOCKER CLI\n                 |\n                 v\n           DOCKER ENGINE\n                 |\n          +------+------+\n          |      |      |\n          v      v      v\n       IMAGES CONTAINERS NETWORKS\n          |\n          v\n       REGISTRY\n```\n\nThe Docker CLI is the command-line interface used to interact with Docker.\n\nFor example:\n\n```bash\ndocker build -t marketplace-api .\n```\n\nThis instructs Docker to build an image.\n\nThe Docker daemon or another configured builder performs the work.\n\n### Check whether Docker is installed\n\nRun:\n\n```bash\ndocker --version\n```\n\nThis displays the installed Docker client version.\n\nExample:\n\n```text\nDocker version 27.x.x\n```\n\nThe exact version depends on your installation.\n\nCheck whether Docker is running:\n\n```bash\ndocker info\n```\n\nThis displays information about the Docker environment.\n\nIf Docker is not running or the client cannot reach the daemon, you may receive a connection error.\n\n## 3. What Is a Dockerfile?\n\nA Dockerfile is a text file containing instructions used to build a Docker image.\n\nIt defines how the image should be constructed.\n\nA Dockerfile commonly specifies:\n\n- The base image.\n- Working directory.\n- Application files.\n- Dependencies.\n- Environment configuration.\n- Exposed ports.\n- Startup command.\n\nA typical Dockerfile:\n\n```dockerfile\nFROM node:22-alpine\n\nWORKDIR /app\n\nCOPY package*.json ./\n\nRUN npm ci\n\nCOPY . .\n\nEXPOSE 3000\n\nCMD [\"node\", \"server.js\"]\n```\n\nThis example builds a Node.js application image.\n\nLet's understand every instruction.\n\n## 4. The FROM Instruction\n\n```dockerfile\nFROM node:22-alpine\n```\n\n`FROM` specifies the base image.\n\nA base image provides the starting filesystem and runtime environment for your application image.\n\nIn this example:\n\n- `node` identifies the official Node.js image repository.\n- `22` identifies the Node.js major version.\n- `alpine` identifies an Alpine Linux-based variant.\n\nWhy do we need a base image?\n\nOur Node.js application requires the Node.js runtime.\n\nInstead of manually installing Node.js into a blank filesystem, we start with an image that already provides it.\n\n### Other base image examples\n\n| Image | Purpose |\n|---|---|\n| `node:22-alpine` | Node.js runtime |\n| `python:3.12-slim` | Python runtime |\n| `nginx:alpine` | Nginx web server |\n| `ubuntu:24.04` | Ubuntu environment |\n| `postgres:17` | PostgreSQL database |\n| `redis:7-alpine` | Redis service |\n\n**Important:** Base image tags should be selected deliberately. For production, pin appropriate versions and consider image digests when reproducibility and supply-chain control are important.\n\n## 5. The WORKDIR Instruction\n\n```dockerfile\nWORKDIR /app\n```\n\n`WORKDIR` sets the working directory inside the image.\n\nIn this case, the application will use:\n\n```text\n/app\n```\n\nas its working directory.\n\nSubsequent instructions such as `COPY`, `RUN`, and `CMD` use this directory as their default working location unless they specify another location.\n\nWhy is this useful?\n\nWithout a clearly defined working directory, application files could be placed in confusing locations.\n\nA structured container filesystem makes maintenance easier.\n\n## 6. The COPY Instruction\n\n```dockerfile\nCOPY package*.json ./\n```\n\n`COPY` transfers files from the build context into the image filesystem.\n\nThe first argument identifies the source.\n\nThe second argument identifies the destination.\n\nIn this example:\n\n- `package*.json` matches package-related JSON files such as `package.json` and `package-lock.json`.\n- `./` represents the current working directory inside the image, which is `/app`.\n\nThe result may look like:\n\n```text\n/app/\n    package.json\n    package-lock.json\n```\n\nWhy copy dependency files first?\n\nBecause Docker can reuse cached build layers when the dependency files have not changed.\n\nWe will explore build caching in Part 2.\n\n## 7. The RUN Instruction\n\n```dockerfile\nRUN npm ci\n```\n\n`RUN` executes a command during image construction.\n\nHere, npm installs the dependencies listed in the package lock file.\n\n`npm ci` is designed for clean, reproducible installation in automated environments.\n\nIt:\n\n- Uses the lock file.\n- Removes an existing `node_modules` directory if present.\n- Installs the locked dependency versions.\n- Fails when the lock file is inconsistent with `package.json`.\n\nWhy use `npm ci` instead of `npm install` in this Dockerfile?\n\nFor a project with a committed `package-lock.json`, `npm ci` helps ensure that builds use the dependency versions recorded in that lock file.\n\n## 8. The Second COPY Instruction\n\n```dockerfile\nCOPY . .\n```\n\nThis copies the remaining files from the build context into the current working directory inside the image.\n\nThe first dot means the current build-context directory.\n\nThe second dot means the current destination directory.\n\nIf your project contains:\n\n```text\nmarketplace-api/\n    package.json\n    package-lock.json\n    server.js\n    routes/\n    controllers/\n```\n\nThe container image may contain:\n\n```text\n/app/\n    package.json\n    package-lock.json\n    server.js\n    routes/\n    controllers/\n```\n\nHowever, this instruction also copies files that should not necessarily enter the image unless you exclude them.\n\nThat is why `.dockerignore` is important.\n\n## 9. The EXPOSE Instruction\n\n```dockerfile\nEXPOSE 3000\n```\n\n`EXPOSE` documents the port that the application is intended to use.\n\nIn this example, the Node.js application listens on port 3000.\n\n**Important:** `EXPOSE` does not publish the port to your host machine and does not automatically configure a firewall.\n\nTo publish a port when starting a container, use:\n\n```bash\ndocker run -p 3000:3000 marketplace-api\n```\n\nThe first `3000` is the host port.\n\nThe second `3000` is the container port.\n\n## 10. The CMD Instruction\n\n```dockerfile\nCMD [\"node\", \"server.js\"]\n```\n\n`CMD` defines the default command executed when a container starts from the image.\n\nIn this example:\n\n- `node` launches the Node.js runtime.\n- `server.js` is the application entry point.\n\nThe JSON-array form is called exec form.\n\nIt is generally preferable for application startup because it runs the executable directly rather than through a shell.\n\nThis helps the application receive operating-system signals correctly.\n\n### Difference between RUN and CMD\n\n| Instruction | When it executes | Purpose |\n|---|---|---|\n| RUN | During image build | Install or configure something |\n| CMD | When a container starts | Define the default runtime command |\n\nFor example:\n\n```dockerfile\nRUN npm ci\n```\n\nInstalls dependencies while building the image.\n\n```dockerfile\nCMD [\"node\", \"server.js\"]\n```\n\nStarts the application when a container is launched.\n\nThese instructions have different responsibilities.\n\n## 11. Other Important Dockerfile Instructions\n\n### ENV\n\nDefines environment variables inside the image.\n\nExample:\n\n```dockerfile\nENV NODE_ENV=production\n```\n\nThis sets the `NODE_ENV` variable to `production`.\n\nThe application can read it through:\n\n```javascript\nconsole.log(process.env.NODE_ENV);\n```\n\nExpected output:\n\n```text\nproduction\n```\n\n**Security warning:** Do not store passwords, API keys, private keys, or database credentials in Dockerfile `ENV` instructions. Values may become visible through image metadata or container inspection. Use runtime secrets management.\n\n### ARG\n\nDefines a build-time variable.\n\nExample:\n\n```dockerfile\nARG APP_VERSION=1.0.0\n```\n\nThis creates a build argument with a default value.\n\nYou can override it during a build:\n\n```bash\ndocker build --build-arg APP_VERSION=2.0.0 -t marketplace-api .\n```\n\nUse `ARG` for build configuration, not secrets.\n\n### USER\n\nDefines the user used for subsequent build instructions and the default runtime process.\n\nExample:\n\n```dockerfile\nUSER node\n```\n\nThis selects the `node` user provided by the official Node.js image.\n\nRunning applications as non-root users reduces the impact of some security vulnerabilities.\n\n### ENTRYPOINT\n\nDefines the executable that starts when the container runs.\n\nExample:\n\n```dockerfile\nENTRYPOINT [\"node\"]\n```\n\nIf combined with:\n\n```dockerfile\nCMD [\"server.js\"]\n```\n\nThe effective default command becomes:\n\n```bash\nnode server.js\n```\n\n`ENTRYPOINT` is commonly used when an image is designed around a particular executable.\n\n### LABEL\n\nAdds metadata to an image.\n\nExample:\n\n```dockerfile\nLABEL maintainer=\"engineering@example.com\"\n```\n\nLabels can describe:\n\n- Application name.\n- Version.\n- Build information.\n- Ownership.\n- Source repository.\n\nAvoid placing private information in labels.\n\n### ADD\n\nCopies files into the image and supports some additional behaviours, including extracting local tar archives.\n\nExample:\n\n```dockerfile\nADD application.tar.gz /app/\n```\n\nFor ordinary file copying, prefer `COPY` because its behaviour is more straightforward.\n\n## 12. Understanding the Docker Build Context\n\nThe build context is the set of files made available to the builder when an image is built.\n\nConsider:\n\n```bash\ndocker build -t marketplace-api .\n```\n\nThe final dot represents the current directory as the build context.\n\nIf your directory is:\n\n```text\nmarketplace-api/\n    Dockerfile\n    package.json\n    package-lock.json\n    server.js\n    routes/\n    .env\n    node_modules/\n```\n\nAll files in the context may be sent to the builder unless excluded by `.dockerignore`.\n\nThis can increase build time and accidentally expose sensitive files.\n\n### Understanding .dockerignore\n\nA `.dockerignore` file tells Docker which files and directories to exclude from the build context.\n\nCreate:\n\n```text\n.dockerignore\n```\n\nAdd:\n\n```text\nnode_modules\n.git\n.env\n.env.*\nnpm-debug.log\ncoverage\ndist\n```\n\nExplanation:\n\n| Entry | Purpose |\n|---|---|\n| `node_modules` | Excludes local dependencies |\n| `.git` | Excludes Git repository data |\n| `.env` | Excludes local environment configuration |\n| `.env.*` | Excludes environment-specific files |\n| `npm-debug.log` | Excludes npm debug logs |\n| `coverage` | Excludes test coverage output |\n| `dist` | Excludes an existing build directory in this example |\n\nOnly exclude directories such as `dist` when your Docker build generates the required output itself or otherwise does not need those files.\n\nThe `.dockerignore` file should be reviewed carefully so that required application files are not accidentally excluded.\n\n## 13. A Complete First Dockerfile\n\nSuppose your Node.js application contains:\n\n```text\nmarketplace-api/\n    Dockerfile\n    .dockerignore\n    package.json\n    package-lock.json\n    server.js\n```\n\nYour `package.json`:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"name\": \"marketplace-api\",\n  \"version\": \"1.0.0\",\n  \"scripts\": {\n    \"start\": \"node server.js\"\n  },\n  \"dependencies\": {\n    \"express\": \"^5.0.0\"\n  }\n}\n```\n\nYour `server.js`:\n\n```javascript\nconst express = require(\"express\");\n\nconst app = express();\n\napp.get(\"/\", (req, res) => {\n    res.send(\"Marketplace API is running\");\n});\n\napp.get(\"/health\", (req, res) => {\n    res.json({ status: \"healthy\" });\n});\n\nconst PORT = process.env.PORT || 3000;\n\napp.listen(PORT, \"0.0.0.0\", () => {\n    console.log(`Server running on port ${PORT}`);\n});\n```\n\n### Explain the application code\n\n```javascript\nconst express = require(\"express\");\n```\n\nImports the Express package.\n\n```javascript\nconst app = express();\n```\n\nCreates an Express application instance.\n\n```javascript\napp.get(\"/\", (req, res) => {\n    res.send(\"Marketplace API is running\");\n});\n```\n\nDefines a GET route for the root URL.\n\nWhen a client requests `/`, the server responds with the text message.\n\n```javascript\napp.get(\"/health\", (req, res) => {\n    res.json({ status: \"healthy\" });\n});\n```\n\nDefines a health endpoint.\n\nIt returns a JSON response that can be used by monitoring systems and load balancers.\n\n```javascript\nconst PORT = process.env.PORT || 3000;\n```\n\nReads the port from an environment variable.\n\nIf no value is provided, it uses 3000.\n\n```javascript\napp.listen(PORT, \"0.0.0.0\", () => {\n```\n\nStarts the HTTP server on the selected port and binds it to all IPv4 interfaces inside the container.\n\nThis is important because binding only to `127.0.0.1` inside the container can prevent access through the container's network interface.\n\n```javascript\nconsole.log(`Server running on port ${PORT}`);\n```\n\nPrints a startup message.\n\n### Dockerfile\n\n```dockerfile\nFROM node:22-alpine\n\nWORKDIR /app\n\nCOPY package*.json ./\n\nRUN npm ci --omit=dev\n\nCOPY --chown=node:node . .\n\nENV NODE_ENV=production\n\nUSER node\n\nEXPOSE 3000\n\nCMD [\"node\", \"server.js\"]\n```\n\nThis version:\n\n- Uses Node.js 22.\n- Creates `/app`.\n- Copies dependency manifests.\n- Installs production dependencies.\n- Copies application files.\n- Sets production mode.\n- Runs as a non-root user.\n- Documents port 3000.\n- Starts the application.\n\nFor this example, a valid `package-lock.json` must exist.\n\n## Part 1 Summary\n\nYou have learned:\n\n- What Docker and containers are.\n- Docker architecture.\n- What a Dockerfile does.\n- Base images.\n- WORKDIR.\n- COPY.\n- RUN.\n- EXPOSE.\n- CMD and ENTRYPOINT.\n- ENV and ARG.\n- USER and LABEL.\n- Build contexts.\n- `.dockerignore`.\n- Creating a complete Node.js Dockerfile.\n\n**Progress: Part 1 of 4**\n\n---\n\n# PART 2 OF 4: IMAGE BUILDS, LAYERS, CACHING AND OPTIMIZATION\n\n## 1. What Is a Docker Image Build?\n\nA Docker image build is the process of converting Dockerfile instructions and build-context files into an image.\n\nFor example:\n\n```bash\ndocker build -t marketplace-api .\n```\n\nLet's break down this command.\n\n| Part | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| `docker` | Docker command-line tool |\n| `build` | Starts an image build |\n| `-t` | Assigns a name or tag |\n| `marketplace-api` | Image repository name |\n| `.` | Current directory as build context |\n\nAfter a successful build, Docker creates an image.\n\nYou can inspect images using:\n\n```bash\ndocker images\n```\n\nExample:\n\n```text\nREPOSITORY          TAG       IMAGE ID\nmarketplace-api     latest    abc123def456\n```\n\nThe image ID is an identifier for the built image.\n\n## 2. Understanding Image Layers\n\nDocker images are constructed from layers.\n\nA layer represents filesystem changes associated with image-building instructions.\n\nConsider:\n\n```dockerfile\nFROM node:22-alpine\n\nWORKDIR /app\n\nCOPY package*.json ./\n\nRUN npm ci\n\nCOPY . .\n\nCMD [\"node\", \"server.js\"]\n```\n\nA simplified view:\n\n```text\n        FINAL IMAGE\n             |\n             v\n       CMD INSTRUCTION\n             |\n             v\n        APPLICATION FILES\n             |\n             v\n       NPM DEPENDENCIES\n             |\n             v\n       PACKAGE FILES\n             |\n             v\n        BASE IMAGE\n```\n\nEach layer builds upon the filesystem provided by previous layers.\n\nThe image consists of these layers and associated image configuration.\n\n### Why are layers useful?\n\nThey allow Docker to reuse unchanged build results.\n\nThis can reduce build time and storage duplication.\n\nFor example, if your application code changes but your dependencies remain the same, Docker may reuse the dependency installation layer.\n\n## 3. Understanding Docker Build Cache\n\nDocker can cache the results of previous build instructions.\n\nConsider:\n\n```dockerfile\nFROM node:22-alpine\n\nWORKDIR /app\n\nCOPY package*.json ./\n\nRUN npm ci\n\nCOPY . .\n\nCMD [\"node\", \"server.js\"]\n```\n\nSuppose you modify only:\n\n```text\nserver.js\n```\n\nThe dependency files remain unchanged.\n\nDocker may reuse:\n\n- Base image layer.\n- Working directory result.\n- Package file copy layer.\n- Dependency installation layer.\n\nIt only needs to process the changed application content and later instructions.\n\nThis makes the build faster.\n\n### Poor Dockerfile ordering\n\nConsider:\n\n```dockerfile\nFROM node:22-alpine\n\nWORKDIR /app\n\nCOPY . .\n\nRUN npm ci\n\nCMD [\"node\", \"server.js\"]\n```\n\nHere, every source code change may invalidate the earlier `COPY . .` instruction.\n\nBecause the dependency installation comes after it, Docker may need to reinstall dependencies even when `package.json` has not changed.\n\n### Better ordering\n\n```dockerfile\nFROM node:22-alpine\n\nWORKDIR /app\n\nCOPY package*.json ./\n\nRUN npm ci\n\nCOPY . .\n\nCMD [\"node\", \"server.js\"]\n```\n\nThis separates dependency installation from application source copying.\n\nThe dependency layer can often be reused.\n\n## 4. Building an Image with a Custom Tag\n\nBuild:\n\n```bash\ndocker build -t marketplace-api:v1 .\n```\n\nThis creates an image tagged `v1`.\n\nThe tag identifies a particular version or build label.\n\nAnother example:\n\n```bash\ndocker build -t marketplace-api:production .\n```\n\nHowever, a tag such as `production` does not automatically make an image suitable for production. It is only a label.\n\n### Build with a version argument\n\nSuppose your Dockerfile contains:\n\n```dockerfile\nARG APP_VERSION=1.0.0\n\nLABEL app.version=$APP_VERSION\n```\n\nBuild:\n\n```bash\ndocker build --build-arg APP_VERSION=2.0.0 -t marketplace-api:v2 .\n```\n\nThis passes the version into the build.\n\nThe resulting image receives a label:\n\n```text\napp.version=2.0.0\n```\n\nInspect it:\n\n```bash\ndocker image inspect marketplace-api:v2\n```\n\nThis displays detailed image metadata.\n\n## 5. Understanding Multi-Stage Builds\n\nMulti-stage builds allow you to use multiple build stages in one Dockerfile.\n\nThey are particularly useful when applications require compilation.\n\nFor example:\n\n- TypeScript.\n- React.\n- Angular.\n- Vue.\n- Go.\n- Java.\n- C and C++.\n\nThe build environment may require tools that the final application does not need.\n\nInstead of including all build tools in the production image, we can use a separate build stage.\n\n### Example: TypeScript application\n\nProject structure:\n\n```text\ntypescript-api/\n    Dockerfile\n    package.json\n    package-lock.json\n    tsconfig.json\n    src/\n        server.ts\n```\n\nA multi-stage Dockerfile:\n\n```dockerfile\nFROM node:22-alpine AS builder\n\nWORKDIR /app\n\nCOPY package*.json ./\n\nRUN npm ci\n\nCOPY tsconfig.json ./\nCOPY src ./src\n\nRUN npm run build\n\n\nFROM node:22-alpine AS production\n\nWORKDIR /app\n\nENV NODE_ENV=production\n\nCOPY package*.json ./\n\nRUN npm ci --omit=dev\n\nCOPY --from=builder /app/dist ./dist\n\nUSER node\n\nEXPOSE 3000\n\nCMD [\"node\", \"dist/server.js\"]\n```\n\n### Stage 1: Builder\n\n```dockerfile\nFROM node:22-alpine AS builder\n```\n\nStarts a build stage named `builder`.\n\n```dockerfile\nWORKDIR /app\n```\n\nSets the working directory.\n\n```dockerfile\nCOPY package*.json ./\n```\n\nCopies the dependency manifests.\n\n```dockerfile\nRUN npm ci\n```\n\nInstalls all dependencies, including development dependencies needed for compilation.\n\n```dockerfile\nCOPY tsconfig.json ./\nCOPY src ./src\n```\n\nCopies the TypeScript configuration and source directory.\n\n```dockerfile\nRUN npm run build\n```\n\nRuns the project's build script.\n\nThis should generate JavaScript files in the `dist` directory, assuming the project is configured that way.\n\n### Stage 2: Production\n\n```dockerfile\nFROM node:22-alpine AS production\n```\n\nStarts a separate final stage.\n\nIt does not automatically include every file from the builder stage.\n\n```dockerfile\nWORKDIR /app\n```\n\nSets the production working directory.\n\n```dockerfile\nENV NODE_ENV=production\n```\n\nSets production mode.\n\n```dockerfile\nCOPY package*.json ./\n```\n\nCopies the package manifests.\n\n```dockerfile\nRUN npm ci --omit=dev\n```\n\nInstalls only production dependencies.\n\n```dockerfile\nCOPY --from=builder /app/dist ./dist\n```\n\nCopies the compiled application from the builder stage.\n\nThis is the most important multi-stage instruction.\n\nIt transfers the compiled output without copying the complete build environment.\n\n```dockerfile\nUSER node\n```\n\nRuns the application as a non-root user.\n\n```dockerfile\nEXPOSE 3000\n```\n\nDocuments the application port.\n\n```dockerfile\nCMD [\"node\", \"dist/server.js\"]\n```\n\nStarts the compiled application.\n\n### Advantages of multi-stage builds\n\n- Smaller production images.\n- Fewer unnecessary build tools.\n- Better separation of build and runtime environments.\n- Reduced unnecessary attack surface.\n- More manageable deployment artifacts.\n\n**Important:** Multi-stage builds do not automatically guarantee a secure or minimal image. You still need to choose appropriate base images, manage dependencies, and review what is copied into the final stage.\n\n## 6. Image Size Optimization\n\nLarge images can increase:\n\n- Build time.\n- Registry storage.\n- Network transfer time.\n- Deployment time.\n- Storage consumption on servers.\n\nConsider two approaches.\n\n### Approach A: Large image\n\n```dockerfile\nFROM node:22\n\nWORKDIR /app\n\nCOPY . .\n\nRUN npm install\n\nCMD [\"node\", \"server.js\"]\n```\n\nThis uses the general Node.js image, copies the complete context, and installs dependencies.\n\n### Approach B: More controlled image\n\n```dockerfile\nFROM node:22-alpine\n\nWORKDIR /app\n\nCOPY package*.json ./\n\nRUN npm ci --omit=dev\n\nCOPY --chown=node:node . .\n\nENV NODE_ENV=production\n\nUSER node\n\nCMD [\"node\", \"server.js\"]\n```\n\nThis approach:\n\n- Uses a smaller base image variant.\n- Installs locked production dependencies.\n- Separates dependency installation from source copying.\n- Runs as a non-root user.\n- Avoids unnecessary files when `.dockerignore` is configured correctly.\n\nHowever, Alpine uses musl libc rather than the glibc environment used by many other Linux distributions. Some native dependencies may need additional configuration or may work more easily with a Debian-based image.\n\nA smaller image is not automatically the best image. Compatibility, security, maintenance, and reproducibility also matter.\n\n## 7. Understanding Image Tags and Versioning\n\nAn image can have multiple tags.\n\nExample:\n\n```bash\ndocker build -t marketplace-api:1.0.0 .\n```\n\nYou can add another tag to the same image:\n\n```bash\ndocker tag marketplace-api:1.0.0 marketplace-api:latest\n```\n\nNow the image has two tags.\n\n| Tag | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| `1.0.0` | Specific version label |\n| `latest` | Conventional default tag |\n\nImportant: `latest` does not necessarily mean newest. It is only a tag name that can point to whichever image was assigned to it.\n\nFor production deployment, prefer explicit version tags or immutable digests.\n\n## 8. Inspecting Image Metadata\n\nRun:\n\n```bash\ndocker image inspect marketplace-api:1.0.0\n```\n\nThis displays detailed information about the image.\n\nYou can inspect:\n\n- Image ID.\n- Architecture.\n- Operating system.\n- Environment variables.\n- Entrypoint.\n- Default command.\n- Exposed ports.\n- Image layers.\n\nDisplay image history:\n\n```bash\ndocker history marketplace-api:1.0.0\n```\n\nThis shows information about image layers and their associated build instructions.\n\nIt can help identify large layers and understand how the image was constructed.\n\n## 9. Docker BuildKit\n\nBuildKit is Docker's modern image-building backend.\n\nIt improves image builds through features such as:\n\n- Better caching.\n- Parallel build execution where possible.\n- More efficient build processing.\n- Advanced build mounts.\n- Improved secret handling during builds.\n\nModern Docker installations commonly use BuildKit by default.\n\nA normal build:\n\n```bash\ndocker build -t marketplace-api .\n```\n\nA Buildx build:\n\n```bash\ndocker buildx build -t marketplace-api .\n```\n\nBuildx provides extended build capabilities, including multi-platform builds.\n\n### Build secrets\n\nSuppose an image build needs a private package registry token.\n\nDo not write the token directly into the Dockerfile.\n\nA BuildKit secret mount can make the secret temporarily available during a build instruction.\n\nExample:\n\n```dockerfile\n# syntax=docker/dockerfile:1\n\nFROM node:22-alpine\n\nWORKDIR /app\n\nCOPY package*.json ./\n\nRUN --mount=type=secret,id=npm_token \\\n    TOKEN=\"$(cat /run/secrets/npm_token)\" && \\\n    npm ci\n\nCOPY . .\n\nCMD [\"node\", \"server.js\"]\n```\n\nThis example demonstrates secret mounting, but the command must actually configure the package manager to use the token for private registry authentication. Merely reading the token does not authenticate npm.\n\nBuild with:\n\n```bash\ndocker buildx build \\\n  --secret id=npm_token,src=./npm_token.txt \\\n  -t marketplace-api .\n```\n\nThe secret is mounted temporarily for that build instruction rather than copied into the resulting image filesystem.\n\nDo not commit `npm_token.txt` to Git.\n\n## 10. Multi-Platform Builds\n\nModern applications may need images for different CPU architectures.\n\nCommon architectures include:\n\n- `amd64` \u2014 commonly used by x86-64 servers.\n- `arm64` \u2014 commonly used by ARM-based systems.\n\nYou can use Docker Buildx:\n\n```bash\ndocker buildx build \\\n  --platform linux/amd64,linux/arm64 \\\n  -t marketplace-api:1.0.0 \\\n  --push .\n```\n\nExplanation:\n\n- `docker buildx build` starts an extended build.\n- `--platform` specifies the target platforms.\n- `linux/amd64` targets Linux on x86-64.\n- `linux/arm64` targets Linux on ARM64.\n- `-t` assigns the image tag.\n- `--push` publishes the resulting multi-platform image to a registry.\n- `.` supplies the build context.\n\nThe registry must be accessible, and the builder must support the requested platforms.\n\n## 11. Build Arguments Versus Environment Variables\n\nThese two instructions have different purposes.\n\n### Build argument\n\n```dockerfile\nARG APP_VERSION=1.0.0\n```\n\nA build argument is primarily used during image construction.\n\n### Environment variable\n\n```dockerfile\nENV NODE_ENV=production\n```\n\nAn environment variable becomes part of the image configuration and is available when the container runs unless overridden.\n\n| Feature | ARG | ENV |\n|---|---|---|\n| Main purpose | Build-time configuration | Runtime configuration |\n| Available during build | Yes | Yes |\n| Available in running container | Not automatically | Yes |\n| Appropriate for secrets | No | No |\n\nDo not assume that an ARG is a safe place for passwords or tokens. Build metadata, logs, and caching can expose sensitive values.\n\n## 12. Common Docker Build Errors\n\n### Error 1: Missing package-lock.json\n\nYou run:\n\n```dockerfile\nRUN npm ci\n```\n\nBut the build fails because there is no valid lock file.\n\nSolution:\n\nGenerate and commit the lock file:\n\n```bash\nnpm install\n```\n\nThen rebuild.\n\n### Error 2: File not found\n\nExample:\n\n```text\nCOPY failed: file not found in build context\n```\n\nPossible causes:\n\n- Incorrect file path.\n- File excluded by `.dockerignore`.\n- Wrong build context.\n- Incorrect capitalization.\n\nRemember that Linux filesystems are generally case-sensitive.\n\n### Error 3: Dependency installation fails\n\nPossible causes:\n\n- Missing lock file.\n- Incorrect Node.js version.\n- Network problems.\n- Private registry authentication.\n- Native dependency requirements.\n\nInspect the build logs rather than immediately changing the base image.\n\n### Error 4: Container starts and exits\n\nPossible causes:\n\n- Application crashes.\n- Incorrect CMD.\n- Missing environment variables.\n- Application listens on the wrong interface.\n- Missing application files.\n\nInspect:\n\n```bash\ndocker logs <container-name>\n```\n\nThis displays the container's standard output and error logs.\n\n## Part 2 Summary\n\nYou have learned:\n\n- Building Docker images.\n- Image tags.\n- Docker layers.\n- Build caching.\n- Dockerfile ordering.\n- Multi-stage builds.\n- Image optimization.\n- Image inspection.\n- BuildKit.\n- Build secrets.\n- Multi-platform builds.\n- ARG versus ENV.\n- Common build errors.\n\n**Progress: Part 2 of 4**\n\n---\n\n# PART 3 OF 4: PRACTICAL DOCKERFILE DEVELOPMENT AND IMAGE BUILDS\n\n## 1. Practical Environment Setup\n\nWe will build and run a real Node.js API.\n\nRequired software:\n\n- Docker Engine or Docker Desktop.\n- Node.js for local development, if desired.\n- A terminal.\n- A text editor.\n\nVerify Docker:\n\n```bash\ndocker --version\n```\n\nCheck Docker availability:\n\n```bash\ndocker info\n```\n\nCreate the project:\n\n```bash\nmkdir marketplace-api\ncd marketplace-api\n```\n\nInitialize a Node.js project:\n\n```bash\nnpm init -y\n```\n\nInstall Express:\n\n```bash\nnpm install express\n```\n\nThese commands create the project structure and install Express.\n\nYour directory should now include:\n\n```text\nmarketplace-api/\n    package.json\n    package-lock.json\n    node_modules/\n```\n\n## 2. Create the Application\n\nCreate:\n\n```text\nserver.js\n```\n\nAdd:\n\n```javascript\nconst express = require(\"express\");\n\nconst app = express();\n\napp.use(express.json());\n\napp.get(\"/\", (req, res) => {\n    res.send(\"Marketplace API is running\");\n});\n\napp.get(\"/health\", (req, res) => {\n    res.json({\n        status: \"healthy\",\n        service: \"marketplace-api\"\n    });\n});\n\napp.post(\"/products\", (req, res) => {\n    const { name, price } = req.body;\n\n    if (!name || typeof price !== \"number\" || price <= 0) {\n        return res.status(400).json({\n            error: \"Valid product name and price are required\"\n        });\n    }\n\n    res.status(201).json({\n        message: \"Product received\",\n        product: {\n            name,\n            price\n        }\n    });\n});\n\nconst PORT = process.env.PORT || 3000;\n\napp.listen(PORT, \"0.0.0.0\", () => {\n    console.log(`Marketplace API listening on ${PORT}`);\n});\n```\n\n### Understand the application\n\n```javascript\nconst express = require(\"express\");\n```\n\nImports Express.\n\n```javascript\nconst app = express();\n```\n\nCreates the application.\n\n```javascript\napp.use(express.json());\n```\n\nEnables JSON request-body parsing for supported requests.\n\nWithout this middleware, `req.body` may not contain the parsed JSON payload.\n\n### Root endpoint\n\n```javascript\napp.get(\"/\", (req, res) => {\n    res.send(\"Marketplace API is running\");\n});\n```\n\nDefines the root GET endpoint.\n\n### Health endpoint\n\n```javascript\napp.get(\"/health\", (req, res) => {\n```\n\nCreates an endpoint used to report basic application health.\n\n```javascript\nres.json({\n    status: \"healthy\",\n    service: \"marketplace-api\"\n});\n```\n\nReturns a JSON response.\n\n### Product endpoint\n\n```javascript\napp.post(\"/products\", (req, res) => {\n```\n\nDefines an endpoint that receives product information.\n\n```javascript\nconst { name, price } = req.body;\n```\n\nExtracts the name and price from the request body.\n\n```javascript\nif (!name || typeof price !== \"number\" || price <= 0) {\n```\n\nValidates the supplied information.\n\nIt checks whether:\n\n- The name is missing or falsy.\n- The price is not a JavaScript number.\n- The price is zero or negative.\n\n```javascript\nreturn res.status(400).json({\n    error: \"Valid product name and price are required\"\n});\n```\n\nReturns HTTP 400 for invalid input.\n\n```javascript\nres.status(201).json({\n```\n\nReturns HTTP 201 to indicate that the request was accepted as a resource-creation operation.\n\nThis demonstration does not save the product to a database. A production implementation would also need stronger validation, authentication, authorization, and persistence.\n\n### Application startup\n\n```javascript\nconst PORT = process.env.PORT || 3000;\n```\n\nUses the environment variable if available, otherwise port 3000.\n\n```javascript\napp.listen(PORT, \"0.0.0.0\", () => {\n```\n\nStarts the application on all IPv4 interfaces inside the container.\n\n## 3. Create the Dockerfile\n\nCreate a file named:\n\n```text\nDockerfile\n```\n\nAdd:\n\n```dockerfile\nFROM node:22-alpine\n\nWORKDIR /app\n\nCOPY package*.json ./\n\nRUN npm ci --omit=dev\n\nCOPY --chown=node:node . .\n\nENV NODE_ENV=production\n\nUSER node\n\nEXPOSE 3000\n\nCMD [\"node\", \"server.js\"]\n```\n\n### Review the instructions\n\n| Instruction | Responsibility |\n|---|---|\n| FROM | Selects Node.js runtime |\n| WORKDIR | Creates/selects application directory |\n| COPY | Copies dependency manifests |\n| RUN | Installs production dependencies |\n| COPY | Copies application source |\n| ENV | Sets production environment |\n| USER | Runs as non-root user |\n| EXPOSE | Documents application port |\n| CMD | Starts the API |\n\n## 4. Create .dockerignore\n\nCreate:\n\n```text\n.dockerignore\n```\n\nAdd:\n\n```text\nnode_modules\n.git\n.env\n.env.*\nnpm-debug.log\ncoverage\n```\n\nThis prevents local dependencies, Git data, and local environment files from being included in the build context.\n\nBecause the Dockerfile runs `npm ci`, the dependencies are installed inside the image.\n\nThis helps avoid copying platform-specific local `node_modules` into the container.\n\n## 5. Build Your First Image\n\nRun:\n\n```bash\ndocker build -t marketplace-api:1.0.0 .\n```\n\nDocker will:\n\n1. Read the Dockerfile.\n2. Process the build context.\n3. Select the Node.js base image.\n4. Set the working directory.\n5. Copy dependency files.\n6. Install production dependencies.\n7. Copy the source code.\n8. Configure the image.\n9. Produce the final image.\n\nThe resulting image is called:\n\n```text\nmarketplace-api:1.0.0\n```\n\n## 6. Inspect the Image\n\nRun:\n\n```bash\ndocker images\n```\n\nYou should see your image in the output.\n\nInspect detailed information:\n\n```bash\ndocker image inspect marketplace-api:1.0.0\n```\n\nInspect the build history:\n\n```bash\ndocker history marketplace-api:1.0.0\n```\n\nThis helps you understand the resulting image and how its layers were created.\n\n## 7. Run Your Container\n\nRun:\n\n```bash\ndocker run -d \\\n  --name marketplace-api \\\n  -p 3000:3000 \\\n  marketplace-api:1.0.0\n```\n\nLet's explain every option.\n\n```bash\ndocker run\n```\n\nCreates and starts a container.\n\n```bash\n-d\n```\n\nRuns the container in detached mode.\n\nThe terminal returns instead of remaining attached to the application process.\n\n```bash\n--name marketplace-api\n```\n\nAssigns the container a readable name.\n\n```bash\n-p 3000:3000\n```\n\nMaps host TCP port 3000 to container TCP port 3000.\n\n```bash\nmarketplace-api:1.0.0\n```\n\nSpecifies the image used to create the container.\n\n## 8. Test Your Container\n\nOpen a browser or use:\n\n```bash\ncurl http://localhost:3000\n```\n\nExpected response:\n\n```text\nMarketplace API is running\n```\n\nTest the health endpoint:\n\n```bash\ncurl http://localhost:3000/health\n```\n\nExpected JSON:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"status\": \"healthy\",\n  \"service\": \"marketplace-api\"\n}\n```\n\nTest product submission:\n\n```bash\ncurl -X POST http://localhost:3000/products \\\n  -H \"Content-Type: application/json\" \\\n  -d '{\"name\":\"Laptop\",\"price\":75000}'\n```\n\nExpected response:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"message\": \"Product received\",\n  \"product\": {\n    \"name\": \"Laptop\",\n    \"price\": 75000\n  }\n}\n```\n\nThe application returns the supplied product information.\n\n## 9. Inspect Container Logs\n\nRun:\n\n```bash\ndocker logs marketplace-api\n```\n\nThis displays application output.\n\nFollow new logs:\n\n```bash\ndocker logs -f marketplace-api\n```\n\nThe `-f` option follows the log stream.\n\nThis is useful when investigating startup failures or runtime errors.\n\n## 10. Inspect a Running Container\n\nRun:\n\n```bash\ndocker ps\n```\n\nThis displays running containers.\n\nTo see all containers, including stopped ones:\n\n```bash\ndocker ps -a\n```\n\nInspect a specific container:\n\n```bash\ndocker inspect marketplace-api\n```\n\nThis provides information about:\n\n- Container configuration.\n- Network settings.\n- Mounted volumes.\n- Environment variables.\n- Image identity.\n- Startup configuration.\n\n## 11. Stop and Remove a Container\n\nStop:\n\n```bash\ndocker stop marketplace-api\n```\n\nThis sends a stop signal and allows the application time to shut down.\n\nRemove:\n\n```bash\ndocker rm marketplace-api\n```\n\nThis removes the stopped container.\n\nThe image remains available.\n\nTo remove the image:\n\n```bash\ndocker rmi marketplace-api:1.0.0\n```\n\nThis removes the image tag and may remove the image data if no other references or containers depend on it.\n\n## 12. Rebuild After Changing Application Code\n\nSuppose you modify:\n\n```javascript\nres.send(\"Marketplace API version 2\");\n```\n\nYou need to rebuild the image:\n\n```bash\ndocker build -t marketplace-api:2.0.0 .\n```\n\nThen create a container from the new image.\n\nFor example:\n\n```bash\ndocker run -d \\\n  --name marketplace-api-v2 \\\n  -p 3001:3000 \\\n  marketplace-api:2.0.0\n```\n\nNotice:\n\n```bash\n-p 3001:3000\n```\n\nThis maps host port 3001 to container port 3000.\n\nThe two containers can run simultaneously if their host ports and other resources do not conflict.\n\n## 13. Practical: Use Environment Variables\n\nSuppose your application listens on port 3000 by default.\n\nYou can override the port at runtime:\n\n```bash\ndocker run -d \\\n  --name marketplace-custom-port \\\n  -p 4000:4000 \\\n  -e PORT=4000 \\\n  marketplace-api:1.0.0\n```\n\nExplanation:\n\n- `-e PORT=4000` sets the environment variable.\n- The application reads `process.env.PORT`.\n- It starts listening on port 4000 inside the container.\n- `-p 4000:4000` publishes that port on the host.\n\nNow test:\n\n```bash\ncurl http://localhost:4000\n```\n\n### Why use runtime environment variables?\n\nThey allow the same image to run with different configurations.\n\nFor example:\n\n| Environment | API configuration |\n|---|---|\n| Development | Development database |\n| Testing | Test database |\n| Staging | Staging database |\n| Production | Production database |\n\nThe image can remain the same while runtime configuration changes.\n\nNever put production credentials directly in the Dockerfile.\n\n## 14. Practical: Inspect the Container Process\n\nRun:\n\n```bash\ndocker top marketplace-api\n```\n\nThis displays processes running inside the container.\n\nYou can also execute a command inside the container:\n\n```bash\ndocker exec -it marketplace-api sh\n```\n\nExplanation:\n\n- `docker exec` executes a command inside an existing running container.\n- `-i` keeps standard input open.\n- `-t` allocates a terminal.\n- `sh` starts a shell.\n\nYou can inspect:\n\n```bash\npwd\n```\n\nThis displays the current working directory.\n\n```bash\nls -la\n```\n\nThis displays files and directories.\n\n```bash\nnode --version\n```\n\nThis displays the Node.js runtime version.\n\nExit:\n\n```bash\nexit\n```\n\n## 15. Practical: Build a Multi-Stage React Frontend\n\nNow consider a frontend application.\n\nA React application commonly needs Node.js and build tools during development and compilation.\n\nBut the final static website can be served by Nginx without including Node.js.\n\nA multi-stage Dockerfile:\n\n```dockerfile\nFROM node:22-alpine AS builder\n\nWORKDIR /app\n\nCOPY package*.json ./\n\nRUN npm ci\n\nCOPY . .\n\nRUN npm run build\n\n\nFROM nginx:alpine AS production\n\nCOPY --from=builder /app/dist /usr/share/nginx/html\n\nEXPOSE 80\n```\n\n### Builder stage\n\n```dockerfile\nFROM node:22-alpine AS builder\n```\n\nCreates the build environment.\n\n```dockerfile\nWORKDIR /app\n```\n\nSets the application directory.\n\n```dockerfile\nCOPY package*.json ./\n```\n\nCopies dependency files.\n\n```dockerfile\nRUN npm ci\n```\n\nInstalls frontend dependencies.\n\n```dockerfile\nCOPY . .\n```\n\nCopies the frontend source.\n\n```dockerfile\nRUN npm run build\n```\n\nBuilds the production frontend.\n\nFor this example, the frontend tool must generate its output in `dist`.\n\n### Production stage\n\n```dockerfile\nFROM nginx:alpine AS production\n```\n\nStarts a new image based on Nginx.\n\n```dockerfile\nCOPY --from=builder /app/dist /usr/share/nginx/html\n```\n\nCopies the generated static files into Nginx's default web directory.\n\n```dockerfile\nEXPOSE 80\n```\n\nDocuments the HTTP port.\n\nThis final image contains the web server and generated frontend files, rather than the full Node.js build environment.\n\nRun it:\n\n```bash\ndocker build -t marketplace-frontend:1.0.0 .\n```\n\nThen:\n\n```bash\ndocker run -d \\\n  --name marketplace-frontend \\\n  -p 8080:80 \\\n  marketplace-frontend:1.0.0\n```\n\nTest:\n\n```bash\ncurl http://localhost:8080\n```\n\nYou should receive an HTTP response containing the frontend's HTML.\n\n## 16. Practical: Tag and Push an Image to a Registry\n\nA container image must be stored somewhere if another server is going to pull it.\n\nA Docker registry stores and distributes images.\n\nExamples:\n\n- Docker Hub.\n- GitHub Container Registry.\n- Amazon Elastic Container Registry.\n- Azure Container Registry.\n- Google Artifact Registry.\n\n### Example: Docker Hub\n\nLog in:\n\n```bash\ndocker login\n```\n\nTag your image:\n\n```bash\ndocker tag marketplace-api:1.0.0 yourusername/marketplace-api:1.0.0\n```\n\nReplace `yourusername` with your actual Docker Hub username.\n\nPush:\n\n```bash\ndocker push yourusername/marketplace-api:1.0.0\n```\n\nThis uploads the image to your registry repository.\n\nOn another server, pull it:\n\n```bash\ndocker pull yourusername/marketplace-api:1.0.0\n```\n\nRun:\n\n```bash\ndocker run -d \\\n  --name marketplace-api \\\n  -p 3000:3000 \\\n  yourusername/marketplace-api:1.0.0\n```\n\nThe second server does not need to rebuild the application if it can access the published image.\n\nIt only needs to pull and run the image.\n\n## 17. Practical: Troubleshoot Common Container Problems\n\n### Problem 1: Port already allocated\n\nYou may see:\n\n```text\nBind for 0.0.0.0:3000 failed:\nport is already allocated\n```\n\nThis means another process or container is already using the host port.\n\nCheck:\n\n```bash\ndocker ps\n```\n\nYou can use another host port:\n\n```bash\ndocker run -d -p 3001:3000 marketplace-api:1.0.0\n```\n\n### Problem 2: Container exits immediately\n\nCheck:\n\n```bash\ndocker ps -a\n```\n\nThen:\n\n```bash\ndocker logs marketplace-api\n```\n\nInvestigate the application error, startup command, missing files, and required environment variables.\n\n### Problem 3: Application works inside container but not externally\n\nCheck:\n\n1. Is the application listening on `0.0.0.0` inside the container?\n2. Is the correct port published?\n3. Is the container running?\n4. Does the host firewall permit traffic?\n5. Does the cloud security group permit traffic?\n6. Is the host address and route correct?\n\n### Problem 4: Image is unexpectedly large\n\nInspect:\n\n```bash\ndocker history marketplace-api:1.0.0\n```\n\nThen review:\n\n- Base image size.\n- Unnecessary copied files.\n- Development dependencies.\n- Build artifacts.\n- Large intermediate files.\n- `.dockerignore`.\n\n## 18. Final Practical Challenge\n\nCreate two images:\n\n1. `marketplace-api`\n2. `marketplace-frontend`\n\nThe API should:\n\n- Provide `/`.\n- Provide `/health`.\n- Accept a product request.\n- Use environment variables.\n- Run as a non-root user.\n\nThe frontend should:\n\n- Build into static files.\n- Use a multi-stage Dockerfile.\n- Run using Nginx.\n\nFor both images:\n\n- Create `.dockerignore`.\n- Build with version tags.\n- Run containers.\n- Test endpoints.\n- Inspect logs.\n- Stop and remove containers.\n- Push the images to a registry.\n\n**Part 3 Summary**\n\nYou have now built and executed actual container images.\n\nYou understand the relationship between Dockerfiles, image builds, containers, runtime configuration, and registries.\n\n**Progress: Part 3 of 4**\n\n---\n\n# PART 4 OF 4: KAI ASSESSMENT \u2014 DOCKERFILES AND BUILDS\n\nWelcome to Kai's assessment.\n\nThis assessment checks your understanding of Dockerfile instructions, image architecture, caching, multi-stage builds, optimization, and practical deployment.\n\nAnswer every question and explain your reasoning.\n\n## SECTION A: MULTIPLE-CHOICE QUESTIONS\n\n**1. What is a Dockerfile?**\n\nA. A database backup.  \nB. A text file containing image-building instructions.  \nC. A Linux firewall.  \nD. A running container.\n\n**2. Which Dockerfile instruction selects the base image?**\n\nA. COPY  \nB. RUN  \nC. FROM  \nD. CMD\n\n**3. Which instruction sets the working directory?**\n\nA. WORKDIR  \nB. EXPOSE  \nC. ENV  \nD. LABEL\n\n**4. Which instruction executes a command during image construction?**\n\nA. CMD  \nB. RUN  \nC. USER  \nD. ENTRYPOINT\n\n**5. Which instruction defines the default command when a container starts?**\n\nA. COPY  \nB. EXPOSE  \nC. CMD  \nD. ARG\n\n**6. What is the purpose of `.dockerignore`?**\n\nA. Start Docker.  \nB. Exclude selected files from the build context.  \nC. Delete containers.  \nD. Publish ports.\n\n**7. What is a Docker image layer?**\n\nA. A network cable.  \nB. A filesystem change or layer contributing to an image.  \nC. A database table.  \nD. A running process.\n\n**8. What is the main advantage of multi-stage builds?**\n\nA. They eliminate the need for Dockerfiles.  \nB. They separate build tools from the final runtime image.  \nC. They automatically create databases.  \nD. They disable networking.\n\n**9. Which command builds an image?**\n\nA. `docker start`  \nB. `docker build`  \nC. `docker stop`  \nD. `docker logs`\n\n**10. Which command lists running containers?**\n\nA. `docker images`  \nB. `docker build`  \nC. `docker ps`  \nD. `docker history`\n\n## SECTION B: CONCEPTUAL QUESTIONS\n\n**11.** Explain the difference between a Docker image and a container.\n\n**12.** Explain the purpose of a base image.\n\n**13.** Differentiate between `RUN` and `CMD`.\n\n**14.** Explain the difference between `ARG` and `ENV`.\n\n**15.** Why is copying dependency files before application source files useful?\n\n**16.** Explain how Docker build caching works.\n\n**17.** What is a multi-stage build, and why is it useful?\n\n**18.** Why should production containers generally avoid running as root?\n\n**19.** Explain why `EXPOSE 3000` does not automatically make port 3000 accessible from the host.\n\n**20.** Why should API keys and database passwords not be written directly into Dockerfiles?\n\n## SECTION C: DOCKERFILE ANALYSIS\n\nStudy the following Dockerfile:\n\n```dockerfile\nFROM node:22-alpine\n\nWORKDIR /app\n\nCOPY package*.json ./\n\nRUN npm ci\n\nCOPY . .\n\nENV NODE_ENV=production\n\nEXPOSE 3000\n\nCMD [\"node\", \"server.js\"]\n```\n\n**21.** Explain the purpose of every instruction.\n\n**22.** Identify two ways to improve this Dockerfile for production.\n\n**23.** Why might `npm ci --omit=dev` be preferable in a production-only Node.js image?\n\n**24.** What happens if the application listens only on `127.0.0.1` inside the container?\n\n**25.** What role does the build context play when Docker executes `COPY . .`?\n\n## SECTION D: PRACTICAL COMMAND QUESTIONS\n\n**26.** Write a command to build an image named `marketplace-api` with tag `1.0.0`.\n\n**27.** Write a command to list all Docker images.\n\n**28.** Write a command to run an image in detached mode and map host port 3000 to container port 3000.\n\n**29.** Write a command to inspect the logs of a container named `marketplace-api`.\n\n**30.** Write a command to stop a container named `marketplace-api`.\n\n**31.** Write a command to remove a stopped container named `marketplace-api`.\n\n**32.** Write a command to inspect the history of an image named `marketplace-api:1.0.0`.\n\n**33.** Write a command to execute a shell inside a running container.\n\n## SECTION E: TROUBLESHOOTING\n\n**34.** A Docker build fails because `package-lock.json` cannot be found. Explain the cause and solution.\n\n**35.** A container starts but exits immediately. Describe how you would investigate it.\n\n**36.** A container works locally but cannot be accessed through the server's public IP. List the network and application checks you would perform.\n\n**37.** Every time you change `server.js`, Docker reinstalls all dependencies. Explain why this might happen and how to improve the Dockerfile.\n\n**38.** Your production image contains Git history, local environment files, and development dependencies. Explain how you would correct the build.\n\n## SECTION F: FINAL ARCHITECTURE CHALLENGE\n\n**39. Scenario:**\n\nYou are deploying a marketplace with:\n\n- React frontend.\n- Node.js backend API.\n- MongoDB database.\n- Nginx reverse proxy.\n\nDesign a containerization strategy.\n\nExplain:\n\n1. Which components need Dockerfiles.\n2. Which base images you would choose.\n3. Which components should use multi-stage builds.\n4. How ports should be configured.\n5. How environment variables should be supplied.\n6. How database credentials should be protected.\n7. How the images should be tagged and distributed.\n\n**40. Advanced Dockerfile challenge**\n\nWrite a production-oriented Dockerfile for a Node.js application that:\n\n- Uses a suitable Node.js base image.\n- Creates `/app`.\n- Copies dependency manifests.\n- Installs production dependencies.\n- Copies application source.\n- Sets `NODE_ENV=production`.\n- Runs as a non-root user.\n- Documents port 3000.\n- Starts `server.js`.\n\nExplain every instruction.\n\n---\n\n# FINAL PROJECT: CONTAINERIZE A PRODUCTION MARKETPLACE\n\nBuild a complete containerization workflow for a marketplace.\n\n## Required components\n\n| Component | Technology | Responsibility |\n|---|---|---|\n| Frontend | React | User interface |\n| Backend | Node.js and Express | Application API |\n| Reverse proxy | Nginx | HTTP routing |\n| Database | MongoDB | Persistent data |\n\n## Project requirements\n\n### 1. Frontend\n\nCreate a Dockerfile that:\n\n- Uses Node.js for compilation.\n- Uses Nginx for serving the production build.\n- Uses a multi-stage build.\n- Excludes unnecessary development files from the final image.\n\n### 2. Backend\n\nCreate a Dockerfile that:\n\n- Uses an appropriate Node.js base image.\n- Installs dependencies using the lock file.\n- Copies application source.\n- Runs as a non-root user.\n- Accepts runtime configuration through environment variables.\n\n### 3. Database\n\nUse an appropriate official MongoDB image.\n\nDo not bake database data into the image.\n\nUse persistent storage and secure authentication configuration.\n\n### 4. Reverse proxy\n\nConfigure Nginx to route requests to the backend service.\n\n### 5. Image management\n\nBuild and tag images using meaningful version numbers.\n\nFor example:\n\n```text\nmarketplace-frontend:1.0.0\nmarketplace-api:1.0.0\n```\n\n### 6. Registry\n\nPush the images to an image registry.\n\nPull them onto a separate server and run them there.\n\n### 7. Security\n\nDemonstrate:\n\n- `.dockerignore`.\n- Non-root application execution.\n- Runtime environment variables.\n- Secure secret handling.\n- Appropriate port exposure.\n- Image versioning.\n\n### 8. Documentation\n\nSubmit:\n\n- Application architecture diagram.\n- Dockerfiles.\n- `.dockerignore` files.\n- Build commands.\n- Container run commands.\n- Registry workflow.\n- Troubleshooting notes.\n\n## FINAL SUBMISSION FORMAT\n\n```text\nDOCKER AND CONTAINERS PROJECT\n\n1. Application Architecture:\n\n2. Frontend Dockerfile:\n\n3. Backend Dockerfile:\n\n4. Database Image Configuration:\n\n5. Nginx Configuration:\n\n6. Docker Ignore Configuration:\n\n7. Image Build Commands:\n\n8. Container Run Commands:\n\n9. Registry Configuration:\n\n10. Security Measures:\n\n11. Troubleshooting:\n\n12. Lessons Learned:\n```\n\n## COMPLETION CHECKLIST\n\n- [ ] Understand Docker architecture.\n- [ ] Explain Dockerfiles.\n- [ ] Select appropriate base images.\n- [ ] Use FROM and WORKDIR.\n- [ ] Understand COPY and RUN.\n- [ ] Use CMD and ENTRYPOINT.\n- [ ] Understand ENV and ARG.\n- [ ] Create `.dockerignore`.\n- [ ] Build Docker images.\n- [ ] Understand image layers.\n- [ ] Use build caching effectively.\n- [ ] Create multi-stage builds.\n- [ ] Optimize image sizes.\n- [ ] Run containers.\n- [ ] Inspect images and container logs.\n- [ ] Manage environment variables.\n- [ ] Push and pull images from registries.\n- [ ] Troubleshoot failed image builds.\n- [ ] Create production-oriented Dockerfiles.\n\n# LESSON COMPLETE\n\n**Part 4 of 4 \u2014 Lesson Complete**\n\n### Recommended next lesson\n\n`Docker and Containers: Container Lifecycle and Runtime Management`\n\nYou will learn how containers are created, started, stopped, restarted, inspected, monitored, resource-limited, and managed in development and production environments.";
